```cshtml
@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "Git Branching Concepts";
}

<main id="tutorials" class="container py-1">
    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- Sidebar Navigation -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- Reading Content Area -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">

            <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
                <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

                    <!-- HEADER -->
                    <header class="mb-4">
                        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">Git Branching Concepts</h2>

                        <p class="lead text-muted">
                            Git branching allows developers to work on different
                            features, bug fixes, and experiments independently
                            without directly affecting the main development branch.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            In this lesson, you will learn what branches are,
                            why developers use them, and how branching supports
                            a collaborative development workflow.
                        </p>
                    </header>
                   

                    <!-- SECTION 1 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">1. What Is a Git Branch?</h4>

                        <p>
                            A Git branch is a lightweight, movable pointer to a
                            commit. It identifies a line of development in your
                            repository.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Branches allow you to develop a new feature or fix a
                            bug separately from the main line of development.
                            When your work is ready, you can integrate it into
                            another branch using a merge or another integration
                            workflow.
                        </p>

                        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">
                            <strong>Simple example:</strong>
                            Imagine your application is working correctly. You
                            want to add a login feature. Instead of changing the
                            main branch directly, you create a separate branch,
                            implement the login feature, test it, and then merge
                            the completed changes.
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SECTION 2 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">2. Why Are Branches Important?</h4>

                        <ul>
                            <li class="mb-2">
                                <strong>Independent development:</strong>
                                Work on new features without directly changing
                                the main branch.
                            </li>

                            <li class="mb-2">
                                <strong>Safer bug fixes:</strong>
                                Isolate a fix and test it before integrating it.
                            </li>

                            <li class="mb-2">
                                <strong>Team collaboration:</strong>
                                Different developers can work on separate branches.
                            </li>

                            <li class="mb-2">
                                <strong>Experimentation:</strong>
                                Try new ideas without immediately affecting
                                the primary development line.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <strong>Organized workflow:</strong>
                                Keep feature development, releases, and fixes
                                separated when the project requires it.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SECTION 3 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">3. Common Types of Branches</h4>

                        <div class="table-responsive">
                            <table class="table table-bordered table-hover align-middle">
                                <thead class="table-light">
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Branch Type</th>
                                        <th>Purpose</th>
                                        <th>Example Name</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>

                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Main Branch</strong></td>
                                        <td>
                                            The primary development or release
                                            branch, depending on the team's workflow.
                                        </td>
                                        <td><code>main</code></td>
                                    </tr>

                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Feature Branch</strong></td>
                                        <td>
                                            Used to develop a new feature independently.
                                        </td>
                                        <td><code>feature/login</code></td>
                                    </tr>

                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Bug-Fix Branch</strong></td>
                                        <td>
                                            Used to correct a specific problem.
                                        </td>
                                        <td><code>fix/login-error</code></td>
                                    </tr>

                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Release Branch</strong></td>
                                        <td>
                                            Used to prepare and stabilize a release
                                            when the team's workflow requires it.
                                        </td>
                                        <td><code>release/1.0</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SECTION 4 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">4. Understanding Branches Visually</h4>

                        <p>
                            Consider a project with a main branch and a separate
                            feature branch. Both branches can point to different
                            commits as development progresses.
                        </p>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            main:     A --- B --- C<br />
                            &nbsp;             \<br />
                            feature:          D --- E
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            In this simplified diagram, commits D and E represent
                            work developed on the feature branch after it diverged
                            from the main branch at commit B.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Once the feature is complete, its changes can be
                            integrated into the main branch.
                        </p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SECTION 5 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">5. Basic Git Branch Commands</h4>

                        <p>
                            Git provides simple commands for creating, listing,
                            and switching branches.
                        </p>

                        <h6 class="fw-bold mt-3">View Existing Branches</h6>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git branch
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            Lists local branches and marks the currently checked-out
                            branch with an asterisk.
                        </p>

                        <h6 class="fw-bold mt-3">Create a New Branch</h6>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git branch feature/login
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            Creates a branch named <code>feature/login</code>
                            without switching to it.
                        </p>

                        <h6 class="fw-bold mt-3">Switch to a Branch</h6>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git switch feature/login
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            Switches your working directory to the selected branch.
                        </p>

                        <h6 class="fw-bold mt-3">Create and Switch in One Command</h6>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git switch -c feature/login
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            Creates the new branch and switches to it immediately.
                        </p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SECTION 6 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">6. A Typical Branching Workflow</h4>

                        <ol>
                            <li class="mb-2">
                                Start from the appropriate base branch.
                            </li>
                            <li class="mb-2">
                                Create a feature branch.
                            </li>
                            <li class="mb-2">
                                Make changes and commit your work.
                            </li>
                            <li class="mb-2">
                                Test your changes and review the code.
                            </li>
                            <li class="mb-2">
                                Push the branch to the remote repository if needed.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Merge the completed work into the target branch
                                according to your team's workflow.
                            </li>
                        </ol>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git switch main<br />
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git pull<br />
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git switch -c feature/login<br />
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git add .<br />
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git commit -m "Add login feature"<br />
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git push -u origin feature/login
                        </div>

                        <p class="text-muted small">
                            These commands assume that the repository has a remote
                            named <code>origin</code>, the main branch is named
                            <code>main</code>, and the changes are ready to commit.
                            Review <code>git status</code> before staging files.
                        </p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SUMMARY -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <div class="alert alert-dark border-0 mb-0">
                            <h5 class="fw-bold mb-2">
                                <i class="bi bi-lightbulb me-2"></i>
                                Key Takeaway
                            </h5>

                            <p class="mb-0">
                                Git branches provide separate lines of development
                                within a repository. They help developers isolate
                                features and bug fixes, collaborate safely, and
                                integrate changes when the work is ready.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <!-- PREVIOUS / NEXT NAVIGATION -->
                    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center border-top pt-4 mt-4">

                        <!-- PREVIOUS TOPIC -->
                        <a href="@Url.Action(
                                    "IgnoringFiles",
                                    "LocalGitWorkflow",
                                    new { area = "GitHub" }
                                 )"
                           class="btn btn-outline-secondary px-3">

                            <span class="me-2">←</span>
                            Previous: .gitignore
                        </a>

                        <!-- NEXT TOPIC -->
                        <a href="@Url.Action(
                                    "ManagingBranches",
                                    "BranchingAndMerging",
                                    new { area = "GitHub" }
                                 )"
                           class="btn btn-primary px-3">

                            Next: Managing Branches
                            <span class="ms-2">→</span>
                        </a>

                    </div>

                </div>
            </div>

        </section>
    </div>
</main>
```